1
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:07,830
This is Software Engineering Radio, the podcast for professional developers on the web at se-radio.net.

2
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:19,190
SE Radio is brought to you by the IEEE Computer Society and by IEEE Software Magazine, online at computer.org/software.

3
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:25,270
Welcome to Software Engineering Radio. I'm your host Gavin Henry, and today my guest is Dave Thomas.

4
00:00:25,270 --> 00:00:31,910
Dave Thomas has been consulting and programming for 50 years and still writes code almost every day.

5
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:41,190
He's an author of The Pragmatic Programmer, one of my personal favorites, especially the 20th edition, The Manifesto for Agile Software Development,

6
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:47,630
Programming Ruby, Agile Web Development with Rails, Programming Elixir, and I'm not finished yet, and more recently Simplicity.

7
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:56,430
He currently runs The Pragmatic Bookshelf, and in his spare time he is looking at better ways of writing OO code. As in, what does that stand for?

8
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:59,910
Object-oriented. Of course.

9
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:01,951
Dave, welcome to Software Engineering Radio. Well, thank you.

10
00:01:01,951 --> 00:01:03,550
I really appreciate being here.

11
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:05,670
It's great to chat.

12
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:09,751
Is there anything missed in that impressive bio of yours, or you happy with it? Well, not really.

13
00:01:09,751 --> 00:01:12,190
I mean, I I do a lot of random stuff, basically.

14
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:17,591
My wife accuses me of just doing the stuff I'm interested in, which is probably true. That's all right.

15
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:26,030
I'm a bit of a magpie when it comes to the things I'm interested in, so I'm never quite sure what I'm going to be attacking on a particular day or month.

16
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,871
That's a great way to live. I like it.

17
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:31,910
So, before we start, I'll mention a small disclaimer.

18
00:01:31,910 --> 00:01:42,270
I'm pretty close to finishing a book I'm writing for your company, The Pragmatic Bookshelf, about Rust and C, so I actually know how some of this stuff works from the author's perspective. So, I just wanted to say that.

19
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:46,110
And what are you doing talking to me and not finishing the book off, huh?

20
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:48,070
Well, probably good point.

21
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:51,592
I was doing it this morning, don't worry. Oh, good. Good.

22
00:01:51,592 --> 00:01:53,350
Anyway, let's begin.

23
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:57,550
So, the show is called Building Ebooks Infrastructure.

24
00:01:57,550 --> 00:01:59,870
So, we need to lay the foundation.

25
00:01:59,870 --> 00:02:02,510
So, I'd like to start with an overview of what an ebook is.

26
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:10,830
So, an ebook is fundamentally just the text of a book in some kind of accessible format to electronics.

27
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:21,430
In the old days, which was 15, 20 years ago, there were probably half a dozen different standards for what made an ebook.

28
00:02:21,430 --> 00:02:24,630
But nowadays, we've pretty much settled on a format called EPUB.

29
00:02:24,630 --> 00:02:31,510
And EPUB is actually I mean, if you have an EPUB file, you can actually just run unzip on it.

30
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:38,110
And if you unzip an EPUB, you'll find that it's a bunch of HTML, a bunch of assets,

31
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:45,430
and then a couple of manifest type files that give it navigation and metadata and all that kind of stuff.

32
00:02:45,430 --> 00:02:49,270
So, whenever you feed an ebook into your reader,

33
00:02:49,270 --> 00:02:54,110
whether that's a Kindle or a Apple Books or whatever it might be,

34
00:02:54,110 --> 00:03:00,710
all it's doing really is unzipping that and then running some kind of browser clone to actually display the content.

35
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:02,949
You mentioned there used to be other formats.

36
00:03:02,949 --> 00:03:06,509
I remember always having to send a.mobi file.

37
00:03:06,519 --> 00:03:10,670
I think you can still download those when you buy a a book from your website.

38
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,190
Amazon has stopped distributing mobis, I believe.

39
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:15,070
Okay, yeah.

40
00:03:15,070 --> 00:03:22,390
I may be wrong about that because now in the old days, you're right, we had to give Amazon a mobi file.

41
00:03:22,390 --> 00:03:30,070
And that was always a real pain because mobis were different in terms of the stuff that they would support to regular EPUBs.

42
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:35,790
So, getting compatible files between mobis and EPUBs was always a challenge.

43
00:03:35,790 --> 00:03:41,151
But eventually, Amazon gave in and switched across. So, now we submit EPUBs to them.

44
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:45,870
And internally, they do change them into something for the Kindle.

45
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:47,670
I don't know exactly how they do that.

46
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:50,750
For all I know, it may well be mobis still.

47
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:53,350
But yeah, we never now see a mobi file.

48
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:58,190
And was that mainly because EPUB was a standard or is a standard and mobi wasn't?

49
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:07,070
Yeah, I think they were constantly playing catch up with the EPUB and frankly it just wasn't worth it because

50
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:19,789
I think initially they went mobi because it gave them the ability to control stuff a bit better on the Kindle, but eventually EPUB does everything mobi does plus a lot more.

51
00:04:19,789 --> 00:04:29,190
It's a way more flexible format and so I think they just said, "Well, why are we fighting a technology war that we don't really get any benefit from?" So, they just switched across.

52
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:31,710
Did they give you any help to render and produce them?

53
00:04:31,710 --> 00:04:33,630
We didn't have to do anything.

54
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:41,150
We just We were always generating mobis and EPUBs as part of our process and so we just basically stopped sending the mobis.

55
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:43,311
I see. And was there a big difference?

56
00:04:43,311 --> 00:04:46,630
It seems to be that they were playing catch up, you said.

57
00:04:46,630 --> 00:04:48,670
I mean, from our point of view, not at all.

58
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:51,990
From consumer point of view, I honestly don't know.

59
00:04:51,990 --> 00:04:56,630
My guess would be over the years, yes, I think it has improved.

60
00:04:56,630 --> 00:05:06,830
So, for example, when you first started generating mobis, they did not support the equivalent of the pre tag, which is the thing that says, "Do not change the spacing."

61
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:12,270
And that meant the code listings were always very, very challenging.

62
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:21,110
So, for a while, we were actually converting code into images and then embedding the images in the EPUB cuz it was the only way we could find to reliably get them to render.

63
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:28,430
There was a whole bunch of stuff like that where mobis just I mean, they were designed for reading fiction basically.

64
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:32,590
There was a Kindle, it's your ticket to the beach and read a book device.

65
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:43,830
And so, anything that we needed that was a little bit out of the norm, we would have to fake out using a whole bunch of tables or a whole bunch of images or something like that.

66
00:05:43,830 --> 00:05:46,990
And now, we don't bother.

67
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:51,470
Now, we just use EPUB and EPUBs are gradually getting closer and closer to the browser.

68
00:05:51,470 --> 00:05:53,830
So, we get richer and richer formatting as we go forward.

69
00:05:53,840 --> 00:06:00,590
Yeah, I think I remember having to double-tap a code listing cuz it was just a big image and then you'd have to zoom around and it was Uh-huh.

70
00:06:00,590 --> 00:06:02,710
pretty painful.

71
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:06,870
And that was painful for you as a reader and also painful for us as a publisher.

72
00:06:06,870 --> 00:06:09,750
And we were constantly bumping into various limits.

73
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:16,510
And things like what happens if a code listing is bigger than the page and that kind of thing.

74
00:06:16,510 --> 00:06:23,510
And did we get before and I guess the mobis would have been bigger in file size cuz you were putting images in them rather than I think they probably were, yeah.

75
00:06:23,510 --> 00:06:29,270
Initially, we started out trying to keep all of them below about 2 meg because I can't remember there was some arbitrary limit someone had.

76
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:31,070
But nowadays, that's not a problem.

77
00:06:31,070 --> 00:06:32,750
So, we again, we don't have to worry about it.

78
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:38,871
And just to clarify before we move on, is a PDF an ebook? I guess it could be.

79
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:44,990
To my mind, an ebook is something which is convenient to read electronically.

80
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,750
PDFs are certainly readable electronically.

81
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:51,230
The question is, are they convenient?

82
00:06:51,230 --> 00:06:56,550
One of the distinguishing factors I think is whether or not it's reflowable.

83
00:06:56,550 --> 00:07:00,710
Now, that means that when I take a browser, right?

84
00:07:00,710 --> 00:07:10,750
If you bring a browser window up and look at a webpage, if you change the size of the browser window, the text grows or shrinks or move expands or whatever to fill the space.

85
00:07:10,750 --> 00:07:12,430
And you don't have to paginate.

86
00:07:12,430 --> 00:07:23,950
You don't get down to the bottom of one page and then, you know, if you're reading an article, hit some magic key and then the whole screen clears and the next page comes up.

87
00:07:23,950 --> 00:07:26,430
So, that's a kind of fixed format where and PDF is a fixed format.

88
00:07:26,430 --> 00:07:29,550
It's page-based.

89
00:07:29,550 --> 00:07:35,790
Whereas, typically, for the kind of books that we're creating and for typical fiction and everything else, you don't want that.

90
00:07:35,790 --> 00:07:45,790
Instead, you want it to be flow-based, which means that as I change the size of the screen or I change the font, then the layout of the text adjusts to suit that.

91
00:07:45,790 --> 00:07:50,750
Would the PDF reader not do that for you, or would that just be They kind of can now.

92
00:07:50,750 --> 00:07:56,270
You can actually tell it to turn off pagination and do that kind of reflowing.

93
00:07:56,270 --> 00:07:58,310
It messes it up more than it gets it right.

94
00:07:58,310 --> 00:08:06,190
And the reason for that is that PDF format internally, it doesn't really consist of a whole bunch of text with instructions around it.

95
00:08:06,190 --> 00:08:14,550
The text is broken up into very, very small chunks, and then position on the page as a small chunk of text.

96
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:22,909
And so, in order to reflow it, what you've really got to do is basically analyze that text and try to work out, well, this is a paragraph, because you don't know.

97
00:08:22,909 --> 00:08:28,430
It's not anything intrinsic in the PDF to say this is a paragraph.

98
00:08:28,430 --> 00:08:36,350
So, it's actually it's tricky to reflow, and they do it, and hats off to them for doing it, but it's not perfect.

99
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:41,709
I did a show with Peter Wyllie and Jeff Jeff Johnson on the 30 years of PDF.

100
00:08:41,709 --> 00:08:43,630
Cuz they helped do all the spec and everything in there.

101
00:08:43,630 --> 00:08:45,430
We did a deep dive on it.

102
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:49,270
All if listeners want to pick up show 532. Well, I'm going to go and listen to that.

103
00:08:49,270 --> 00:08:51,190
That's interesting.

104
00:08:51,190 --> 00:08:57,431
We speak about the biggest PDF in the world that covers the whole city of Japan, and all sorts of really cool stuff. Mhm.

105
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:00,510
All right.

106
00:09:00,510 --> 00:09:10,470
So, I think you were hinting at this, but do you think books are evolving toward more like a website where you can get a book updated over time, either get it corrected or changed?

107
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:12,710
Well, our books have always been like that.

108
00:09:12,710 --> 00:09:19,030
If you buy an ebook from us, then we will actually send you updates.

109
00:09:19,030 --> 00:09:23,670
And either if we find bugs, sometimes if the author does like a new revision.

110
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:27,590
Typically not for like a second edition, because that's typically a rewrite.

111
00:09:27,590 --> 00:09:35,829
But if an author wants to do like a third or fourth printing, and they discover they want to add another couple of paragraphs, we just throw those in there and distribute it.

112
00:09:35,829 --> 00:09:41,830
And so, you just you get a notification, you download the most recent one, and you have it.

113
00:09:41,830 --> 00:09:44,430
Remember that ebooks are typically designed to be read offline.

114
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:49,510
They may not be, but they're designed that way.

115
00:09:49,510 --> 00:09:51,750
And so we can't just magically update it in your reader.

116
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:53,830
And in fact, you wouldn't want to.

117
00:09:53,830 --> 00:10:00,030
Quite often readers don't want you to do that because they'll be making annotations or whatever in the existing one.

118
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:02,630
So we make them always available.

119
00:10:02,630 --> 00:10:13,310
In a way, I think the biggest thing I'm seeing though is that we're getting more adventurous in what we can do inside a book.

120
00:10:13,310 --> 00:10:15,190
So it's actually not many people know this,

121
00:10:15,190 --> 00:10:27,430
but you know you have disclosure lists in HTML where you can have a heading and you click on it and then the text below it expands and you get a whole bunch of backup material for that.

122
00:10:27,430 --> 00:10:31,110
Yeah, like a I think in the JavaScript sort of widget, well, they call them accordions, don't they?

123
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,711
Accordions, yeah, but in fact that's built into HTML. Oh, okay.

124
00:10:34,711 --> 00:10:40,230
It's doesn't have to have any JavaScript running at all.

125
00:10:40,230 --> 00:10:43,430
And a lot of ebook readers actually support that.

126
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:48,870
And way back when we actually experimented with putting that into our ebooks.

127
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:56,990
So you could actually have like a summary list which if you clicked on a particular link, that would expand to show you the detail.

128
00:10:56,990 --> 00:11:04,350
And at the time we ran into the problem that because most ebook readers back then were e-ink displays,

129
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:09,030
then it basically had to clear the screen and redraw it with the expanded text.

130
00:11:09,030 --> 00:11:10,750
And people just found that too distracting.

131
00:11:10,750 --> 00:11:20,670
But that's an example of the kind of thing that you can do in an ebook that you can't do in most other book style output, and certainly not in paper.

132
00:11:20,670 --> 00:11:28,951
Yeah, it sounds like you could enrich the page more and keep relevant text but not immediately present it. Yeah, absolutely.

133
00:11:28,951 --> 00:11:35,190
And we kind of do that with footnotes now in our ePubs.

134
00:11:35,190 --> 00:11:42,390
A footnote is a link and you tap the link and it goes to the footnote and then on the footnote you tap another link and it takes you back to where you were in the text.

135
00:11:42,390 --> 00:11:44,470
And yeah, that kind of thing is definitely possible.

136
00:11:44,470 --> 00:11:55,470
One of the things that we have discovered though is that one of the attributes of a book is that it is kind of what's the word?

137
00:11:55,470 --> 00:11:59,750
Boring in terms of the way it's laid out and everything else.

138
00:11:59,750 --> 00:12:02,230
You know what to expect when you have a book.

139
00:12:02,230 --> 00:12:12,270
And so if you were reading a book and it suddenly went all psychedelic on you or all the fonts changed or something, that would be really distracting.

140
00:12:12,270 --> 00:12:14,190
And a book is not supposed to be a distraction.

141
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:20,310
A book is simply a substrate where an author can get content to a reader.

142
00:12:20,310 --> 00:12:27,950
And once you start making it all gee whiz, look at the technology, you can kind of get in the way of that communication.

143
00:12:27,950 --> 00:12:38,910
That breaks my heart because I love experimenting with the kind of wild stuff you can do inside an e-pub, but it's just too risky to be honest with you to do that.

144
00:12:38,910 --> 00:12:41,470
People will complain if you do that.

145
00:12:41,470 --> 00:12:45,350
Yeah, it's a sort of medium to disappear into, isn't it? And Exactly right.

146
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:50,031
and no distractions, unlike the rest of the world. Exactly.

147
00:12:50,031 --> 00:12:54,670
Yeah, I mean the last thing you want is a pop-up ad in an e-book.

148
00:12:54,670 --> 00:12:56,470
I think they would try if they Oh, I mean you can do it.

149
00:12:56,470 --> 00:13:01,311
You can actually do it, but yeah, that would be disastrous. Cool.

150
00:13:01,311 --> 00:13:05,870
So, I'm going to take us on to um middle part of our chat today.

151
00:13:05,870 --> 00:13:15,991
So, now that we know what an e-book is and the formats that you support as a publisher and pretty much everyone supports, which is e-pub, like you said, let's talk about building them. Okay.

152
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,710
So, building e-pubs.

153
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:24,551
First question, what do authors write books in? Word?

154
00:13:24,551 --> 00:13:26,190
No. And that's actually, you know what?

155
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:30,390
It may be easier to do this slightly differently and go back and look at the history.

156
00:13:30,390 --> 00:13:34,430
Go because that actually informs a lot of what we do.

157
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:44,990
So, the whole book business started in 1997, I guess, when Andy Hunt and I started putting together The Pragmatic Programmer.

158
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,270
Except we weren't doing The Pragmatic Programmer at the time.

159
00:13:48,270 --> 00:13:57,550
We were just doing a set of notes that we could give our clients because we would turn up at a client, we were doing consulting at the time,

160
00:13:57,550 --> 00:14:04,790
we'd turn up at a client and we'd always spend the first couple of days saying the same things to every client.

161
00:14:04,790 --> 00:14:14,950
Stuff like, maybe you want to test that before you ship it, or can you build this automatically, and all that kind of pretty obvious stuff, but at the time it wasn't obvious.

162
00:14:14,950 --> 00:14:21,910
So, we put together some notes and they were just HTML pages that we were going to print out.

163
00:14:21,910 --> 00:14:33,470
And it was actually my wife who said, "Why don't you see if you can get a publisher in this is Nexus good stuff." And so, we thought, "Well, no, there's no way people would be interested in that as a book."

164
00:14:33,470 --> 00:14:42,550
But, we came up with this cunning plan, which was we would submit it to who we thought was the best publisher for this kind of material and they would reject it,

165
00:14:42,550 --> 00:14:49,070
but when they rejected it, they would say why they rejected it and that would help us to make it better.

166
00:14:49,070 --> 00:14:54,470
So, we submitted it to Addison-Wesley and they screwed us over by accepting it.

167
00:14:54,470 --> 00:14:56,870
And so, we were stuck with having to produce this book.

168
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,550
And we already had a whole bunch of content.

169
00:15:00,550 --> 00:15:07,710
Andy and I uh really strong believers in the power of plain text.

170
00:15:07,710 --> 00:15:10,350
So, text that has basically just words, just characters.

171
00:15:10,350 --> 00:15:16,790
So, we'd never ever used word processors to do anything.

172
00:15:16,790 --> 00:15:20,030
Back then, 1997-98, that was basically Microsoft Word.

173
00:15:20,030 --> 00:15:29,990
And Word has got better, but Word does not produce the kind of quality layout that you need for a book.

174
00:15:29,990 --> 00:15:38,510
So, back then, if you were an author, the chances were pretty good you would be authoring in Microsoft Word, and you would submit your book to your publisher.

175
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:45,750
And the publisher would take your Word document, and they would import it into their own layout software.

176
00:15:45,750 --> 00:15:47,990
And that might be something like FrameMaker.

177
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,350
Nowadays, it would be InDesign.

178
00:15:50,350 --> 00:15:52,030
Whatever it might be.

179
00:15:52,030 --> 00:15:54,550
And that's a one-way process.

180
00:15:54,550 --> 00:16:04,670
They ingest it, it gets into their tool, and at that point, the master copy of the book is in their hands, and they do whatever they have to do to make it look good.

181
00:16:04,670 --> 00:16:10,150
So, now I'm an author, and I have discovered an error in the book, and I want to fix it.

182
00:16:10,150 --> 00:16:14,670
Well, I cannot fix the Word document anymore, because that's now irrelevant.

183
00:16:14,670 --> 00:16:16,350
It's not anything to do with the book anymore.

184
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:28,750
So, what I have to do is to basically send my changes to the publisher as a set of "On page 27, line four, change the word this to that."

185
00:16:28,750 --> 00:16:38,030
And then, some poor person at the other end has to go through, read those instructions, bring up the FrameMaker, and make the whatever the adjustment is.

186
00:16:38,030 --> 00:16:41,350
And to us, that just felt like this impossible, we're never going to do that.

187
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:49,710
So, we convinced Addison-Wesley to let us give them a camera-ready PDF.

188
00:16:49,720 --> 00:16:53,350
Actually, it was a PostScript file at the time.

189
00:16:53,350 --> 00:16:56,950
And we asked them for their spec, and they gave us their spec for typesetting.

190
00:16:56,950 --> 00:17:04,790
And then, we set about putting together a tool chain that would let us write plain text, and have it end up being a book.

191
00:17:04,790 --> 00:17:06,870
And we went through a couple of iterations.

192
00:17:06,870 --> 00:17:09,829
The first one, we used a tool called troff, t r o f f.

193
00:17:09,839 --> 00:17:14,110
That's t r o f f.

194
00:17:14,110 --> 00:17:17,470
And troff was, at that point, easily 20 years old.

195
00:17:17,470 --> 00:17:24,031
It was part of the Unix document as workbench that came out of Bell Labs. And in troff,

196
00:17:24,040 --> 00:17:33,270
what you did is you basically typed your text and then you inserted various formatting instructions by basically you would start a line

197
00:17:33,270 --> 00:17:44,190
with a period a full stop and then you would put like a one or two character instruction like this is a title or this is a page break or something.

198
00:17:44,190 --> 00:17:48,590
And it would then take your text and format it using those instructions.

199
00:17:48,590 --> 00:17:54,190
Kind of like HTML almost with your title Kind of like HTML way more restricted and way more specialized.

200
00:17:54,190 --> 00:17:59,070
But the thing that made it usable is that it had a macro system.

201
00:17:59,070 --> 00:18:02,310
So you could define commands using the lower level commands.

202
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:07,710
So we produced this library that would do the layout and it kind of worked.

203
00:18:07,710 --> 00:18:10,150
But troff was written for academic papers.

204
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:12,510
It wasn't written for book type stuff.

205
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:23,030
And a lot of people have written the books in troff, but honestly it doesn't do a very good job of paragraph breaking where it decides where to put a new line in a paragraph.

206
00:18:23,030 --> 00:18:27,310
It just looks a bit ugly.

207
00:18:27,310 --> 00:18:34,830
So we switched across to using TeX which was Don Knuth's layout system which makes way way nicer output.

208
00:18:34,830 --> 00:18:41,150
And so we produced a set or I produced a set of LaTeX macros to define the book.

209
00:18:41,150 --> 00:18:53,110
And we wrote the book in LaTeX and we delivered the PDFs to Addison-Wesley and to our surprise they just said, "Yep, that's good." So the first edition of Pragmatic Programmer was produced that way.

210
00:18:53,110 --> 00:18:58,311
But the nice thing was I think the first edition I can't remember how many printings we had. It was a lot.

211
00:18:58,311 --> 00:19:09,830
And they would come to us and say, "Any changes you want to make?" And we actually had the ability just to go through the book and update stuff and then regenerate the PDF and send it to them.

212
00:19:09,830 --> 00:19:11,870
It was kind of interesting at the time.

213
00:19:11,870 --> 00:19:20,270
What happened was you would send them the PDF, they would run it through something called a rip, which converts it into a high resolution set of images,

214
00:19:20,270 --> 00:19:27,990
and then they would burn those images onto metal plates, and those metal plates would then go into the printing presses,

215
00:19:27,990 --> 00:19:37,390
and you've probably seen the things on TV, these massive big machines with big drums that are spinning, and paper flies through, goes in white, comes out with text on it.

216
00:19:37,390 --> 00:19:42,350
So, there's actually a decent cost associated with converting a PDF into those metal plates.

217
00:19:42,350 --> 00:19:47,150
So, what they tried to do is to reuse plates that haven't changed.

218
00:19:47,150 --> 00:19:55,831
And so, we had to come up with a system where we could automatically tell them which pages had changed and which pages hadn't to reduce their costs. But, that was fun.

219
00:19:55,831 --> 00:19:57,990
And that was groundbreaking for that time.

220
00:19:57,990 --> 00:19:59,791
I don't know, maybe. I don't know.

221
00:19:59,791 --> 00:20:02,350
Here's the thing, we didn't know what we were doing.

222
00:20:02,360 --> 00:20:06,910
We were just doing what sounded like a good idea at the time.

223
00:20:06,910 --> 00:20:11,630
And so, we were experimenting, and we just doing what we thought was a good idea.

224
00:20:11,630 --> 00:20:16,390
So, thanks for the history of how you got started in text.

225
00:20:16,390 --> 00:20:20,110
So, that answers our question if things are written in Word or not.

226
00:20:20,110 --> 00:20:22,310
I guess other publishers might write in Word.

227
00:20:22,310 --> 00:20:25,470
In technical publishing, that used to be the case.

228
00:20:25,470 --> 00:20:28,590
Nowadays, some don't.

229
00:20:28,590 --> 00:20:31,150
Some still do take Word, but Word is just seems like such a crazy thing.

230
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:34,870
It's really not suited for writing books.

231
00:20:34,870 --> 00:20:40,190
Added to which, if you've ever tried writing a 500-page Word document, it doesn't like that at all.

232
00:20:40,190 --> 00:20:42,990
All right, it bogs down, and it's pretty ugly.

233
00:20:42,990 --> 00:20:53,150
So, when you write a book for The Pragmatic Programmer, does an author use like a what you see is what you get editor, or what what is the actual text format?

234
00:20:53,150 --> 00:21:01,550
No. So, in the intermediate days, we switched from using LaTeX to using an XML-based markup.

235
00:21:01,550 --> 00:21:04,390
Basically, it was a simplified HTML.

236
00:21:04,390 --> 00:21:10,070
And so, you would write in this HTML thing, we called it PML for pragmatic markup language.

237
00:21:10,070 --> 00:21:13,110
And it was semantic markup.

238
00:21:13,110 --> 00:21:18,510
So, you would have tags for here's some code, here's a sidebar, whatever it might be.

239
00:21:18,510 --> 00:21:20,670
And that's what you would write.

240
00:21:20,670 --> 00:21:28,630
And authors had the ability then to be able to run our tool chain on their local computer and generate either a PDF or an EPUB.

241
00:21:28,630 --> 00:21:31,310
And they could then look at their output that way.

242
00:21:31,320 --> 00:21:36,310
So, the answer is no, they don't write in a WYSIWYG format.

243
00:21:36,310 --> 00:21:38,350
And there's two reasons for that.

244
00:21:38,350 --> 00:21:44,830
First of all, I always like there's a quote from Brian Kernighan who said, "WYSIWYG really should be WYSIWAG.

245
00:21:44,830 --> 00:21:49,710
It's what you see is all you get." So, there's no additional semantic information in there.

246
00:21:49,710 --> 00:21:54,950
And that's we wanted to have that extra content.

247
00:21:54,950 --> 00:22:02,350
But secondly, if you give an author a tool that lets them generate a WYSIWYG tool, then what they tend to do is focus on that layout.

248
00:22:02,350 --> 00:22:07,790
And they'll spend a long time twiddling with margins and fonts and everything else.

249
00:22:07,790 --> 00:22:09,830
And that's not what we want.

250
00:22:09,830 --> 00:22:14,510
What we want is authors to focus on their content because we have people to do the layout.

251
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:22,190
And so, it's actually I think a benefit not to be able to see the actual laid out text as you're typing it.

252
00:22:22,190 --> 00:22:27,670
So, in the old days, we used to have an XML based layout called PML.

253
00:22:27,670 --> 00:22:34,990
And authors would write in this PML and then the tool chain would convert that into, you know, PDFs or ebooks or whatever.

254
00:22:34,990 --> 00:22:42,031
We have moved across to using markdown as our input. And it's a work in progress.

255
00:22:42,031 --> 00:22:49,030
We started off by allowing people to embed markdown inside their PML documents.

256
00:22:49,030 --> 00:22:59,630
And in the last year or so, we now encourage authors to write in.md files, markdown files.

257
00:22:59,630 --> 00:23:09,550
And you can still use your old PML tags inside that markdown if you want to, but we're basically slowly moving to the point where you no longer have to.

258
00:23:09,550 --> 00:23:15,750
And so, we actually have a markdown that's rich enough to let you express everything you need to do, not just as an author of a book,

259
00:23:15,750 --> 00:23:23,950
but also as a publisher of a book because there's lots of little small subtle things we have to do in the background to control the process of it.

260
00:23:23,950 --> 00:23:27,590
So, has markdown evolved since you created PML?

261
00:23:27,590 --> 00:23:31,190
Markdown didn't even exist when we started creating PML, I don't think.

262
00:23:31,190 --> 00:23:36,350
Back then it was Textile was the equivalent of markdown.

263
00:23:36,350 --> 00:23:41,790
So, yeah, markdown has, well, first of all, it's become the de facto plain text text writing language,

264
00:23:41,790 --> 00:23:49,430
but also over the years the practicality of markdown has expanded.

265
00:23:49,430 --> 00:23:58,070
And, for example, the ability to embed HTML inside markdown in a kind of rational way means that you don't have to be limited by just a few things that markdown can do.

266
00:23:58,080 --> 00:24:08,590
And a consensus is being gradually reached on how you write extensions for markdown.

267
00:24:08,590 --> 00:24:13,470
And so that we can add extra facilities to our markdown in a kind of consistent way.

268
00:24:13,470 --> 00:24:15,790
So, yeah, markdown has matured a lot.

269
00:24:15,790 --> 00:24:18,150
And, like I say, it's what I write all my books in now.

270
00:24:18,150 --> 00:24:22,791
I want to ask a few technical questions about how you handle markdown then. Sure.

271
00:24:22,791 --> 00:24:30,790
How errors So, you've got your own tool chain that you've written that I don't know if it's still bits of it from when you originally did it and Oh, yeah.

272
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:34,230
Yeah.

273
00:24:34,230 --> 00:24:37,952
So, how do you handle errors in that raw format? Mhm. As well as we can.

274
00:24:37,960 --> 00:24:42,430
So, let me talk you a little bit how the tool chain actually works.

275
00:24:42,430 --> 00:24:45,390
So, what we have is a pipeline.

276
00:24:45,390 --> 00:24:48,990
So, at one end of it you have a set of markdown files.

277
00:24:48,990 --> 00:24:53,030
And one of those markdown files will be called book.md.

278
00:24:53,040 --> 00:25:01,550
And it will typically include all of the different markdown files that make up your book.

279
00:25:01,550 --> 00:25:04,630
We encourage authors to do at least a separate chapter per file.

280
00:25:04,630 --> 00:25:11,510
Some authors actually have each section as a file or whatever, it doesn't really matter.

281
00:25:11,510 --> 00:25:23,430
So, you feed that markdown top-level file into a pipeline and the pipeline goes through it's a variable number depending on the author and the book, but at least half a dozen different preprocessors.

282
00:25:23,430 --> 00:25:34,990
The first one is the one that goes through and takes that book.pml and it goes through and looks for all these inclusions and replaces them with the actual contents of the files.

283
00:25:34,990 --> 00:25:40,030
And that produces a stream of text that doesn't no longer has includes in it.

284
00:25:40,040 --> 00:25:46,310
That stream gets passed to the next thing and the next thing and the next thing.

285
00:25:46,310 --> 00:25:52,910
At some point in that process, we convert the markdown into XML and not coincidentally,

286
00:25:52,910 --> 00:25:58,950
the XML we convert it into is actually our original PML format.

287
00:25:58,950 --> 00:26:13,070
And the reason for that is that we have tens of thousands of lines of code that know how to handle that PML format to generate books in PDF, mobi, epub, whatever formats.

288
00:26:13,070 --> 00:26:20,110
And so by using that as our intermediate target, we can reuse a whole bunch of our tooling as we go forward.

289
00:26:20,110 --> 00:26:28,110
It also means that we can do a more gradual switch over to markdown because authors can still embed PML markup in their markdown documents and

290
00:26:28,120 --> 00:26:36,150
that just makes it all the way through the various stages in the pipeline until it gets to the PML converter.

291
00:26:36,150 --> 00:26:46,510
So, if we're targeting an ebook, what we have to do is convert your PML into XHTML, a 1999 standard.

292
00:26:46,510 --> 00:26:53,590
If we're converting it to a PDF, then what we actually do is we convert it in a couple of steps which we'll talk about in a minute.

293
00:26:53,590 --> 00:27:03,110
And to do that conversion from one format of XML to HTML, we use a fantastic technology called XSLT,

294
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:16,190
which is a way of specifying a set of transforms that you apply to an XML document, which sounds terribly terribly technical.

295
00:27:16,190 --> 00:27:18,790
But, all it really means is you give it a set of rules.

296
00:27:18,790 --> 00:27:31,190
You say to it, "Whenever you see a code tag, for example, replace it with a pre and then whatever the content of that code tag is."

297
00:27:31,190 --> 00:27:41,470
And so, you actually write this very functional style set of templates that translate your XML into whatever format you want.

298
00:27:41,470 --> 00:27:44,630
So, for the ebooks, that is purely That's all we do.

299
00:27:44,630 --> 00:27:56,750
We have some XSLT that takes the input document and generates literally all of the files that are necessary to create, say, an epub.

300
00:27:56,750 --> 00:27:58,430
So, we're generating each of the It's not even chapters.

301
00:27:58,430 --> 00:28:08,270
We have to break the book down into chunks because epub readers need each section to be no longer than, I don't know, three or four pages.

302
00:28:08,270 --> 00:28:10,310
You can't have like the entire book in a single chunk.

303
00:28:10,310 --> 00:28:16,110
So, we break it into chunks, we set them out to separate files, we do all the cross-referencing, we generate the manifests.

304
00:28:16,110 --> 00:28:18,710
All of that stuff is done in XSL.

305
00:28:18,710 --> 00:28:23,710
The only additional code that we have is the code that normalizes our images.

306
00:28:23,710 --> 00:28:29,030
Some ebook readers insist that you only give them black and white images, for example, so we have to convert those.

307
00:28:29,040 --> 00:28:36,910
If we are generating PDFs, we go through a similar path but with a different outcome.

308
00:28:36,910 --> 00:28:44,790
We still use XSLT, but then what we do is we generate another XML format, which is called XSL:FO.

309
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:52,790
And the FO stands for formatting objects.

310
00:28:52,790 --> 00:28:57,710
And FO is frankly, I'm amazed it works because what it takes on is incredibly complicated.

311
00:28:57,710 --> 00:29:06,910
It's basically a way of describing text laid out in two dimensions and is also a way of describing the structure of a book

312
00:29:06,910 --> 00:29:12,950
so that the book effectively is a container for the text that it's creating.

313
00:29:12,950 --> 00:29:20,790
It is still to me a miracle that it actually works, but it does and it produces gorgeous looking PDFs.

314
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:25,990
So that is the essence of the tool chain.

315
00:29:25,990 --> 00:29:35,550
It runs, like I say, as a pipeline and the pipeline varies per book because different authors have different things they want to be able to do.

316
00:29:35,550 --> 00:29:40,150
So for example, some authors want to be able to include mathematical equations in their books.

317
00:29:40,150 --> 00:29:42,590
And we have a preprocessor that will do that.

318
00:29:42,590 --> 00:29:48,390
But if you don't have math in your book, there's no point in slowing down the build by including that preprocessor.

319
00:29:48,390 --> 00:29:50,430
So those kind of things are optional.

320
00:29:50,430 --> 00:30:01,070
So in total, we probably have, oh, I don't know, 20 different preprocessors that people something the author needs to pick to pre process or is that detected at the start?

321
00:30:01,070 --> 00:30:08,950
When you sign up, you get to check out your repository and that's preloaded with the ones that just about everybody uses.

322
00:30:08,950 --> 00:30:19,270
And then your editor will say, you know, you'll say to the editor, "Gosh, I really wish I could do an equation here." And they'll go, "Oh, okay, that's easy." And I'll show you how to do that by adding whatever the preprocessor is to make it happen.

323
00:30:19,270 --> 00:30:20,990
I see.

324
00:30:20,990 --> 00:30:24,030
And if it does, getting back to the question, was how are errors handled?

325
00:30:24,030 --> 00:30:25,791
I guess Oh, that's right. I'm sorry.

326
00:30:25,791 --> 00:30:27,470
You're absolutely right.

327
00:30:27,470 --> 00:30:36,790
So the problem we have there is it's exactly the same as a compiler or something and that is by the time you're sort of six or seven levels deep in preprocessors,

328
00:30:36,790 --> 00:30:42,950
you've lost all of the original information about where you were in the input document.

329
00:30:42,950 --> 00:30:48,310
And so, what we do, and it's not perfect, but when the includer runs,

330
00:30:48,310 --> 00:30:59,750
the first thing it does is it adds XML comments to the stream every time it includes a file, and that includes the file name and a bit of other information.

331
00:30:59,760 --> 00:31:11,070
And then every preprocessor that changes the text as it goes through will flag the start and end of that change with another comment to say,

332
00:31:11,070 --> 00:31:15,390
"I am at the relative line 27 into the current chunk."

333
00:31:15,400 --> 00:31:27,870
And so, at some point, if you get an error, what it then does is it goes back through that kind of stack of location things to work out where you actually were in the original document.

334
00:31:27,870 --> 00:31:30,910
And it tries to report it.

335
00:31:30,910 --> 00:31:40,230
Typically, it can get it within a few lines, unless you're looking at a really, really long run of text,

336
00:31:40,230 --> 00:31:46,070
in which case you may be off like a dozen lines because there's none of these little flags to try to record where you were in the original.

337
00:31:46,070 --> 00:31:54,150
So, it's definitely a problem, and it's something I'm trying to working on refining always, but it's getting better than it was.

338
00:31:54,150 --> 00:32:04,231
And you've already answered one of my questions, which was going to be about equations, so they'll be enabled at the start manually whether you're going to do LaTeX or something like that. Right.

339
00:32:04,231 --> 00:32:06,990
How are links and references handled within the book?

340
00:32:06,990 --> 00:32:17,310
So, that is actually an ongoing question because if you think about it, if you're in a PDF, then imagine you're just reading on paper.

341
00:32:17,310 --> 00:32:19,190
You know, there's no hyperlinks.

342
00:32:19,190 --> 00:32:21,590
You can't press on the paper and it turns the page for you.

343
00:32:21,590 --> 00:32:33,190
So, when you do a cross reference in a paper book, what you have to do is say, "As you can see in the section on, you know, editing a file on page 27, you do this."

344
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:41,950
So, a cross reference there is basically a verbose description of where you're pointing to.

345
00:32:41,950 --> 00:32:46,390
If it's a ebook, then the same thing can just be a hyperlink, and you click on it and it takes you there.

346
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:54,591
And what we wanted was single a single markup that would let you express both of those. And we're very close.

347
00:32:54,591 --> 00:33:03,550
There's some cases where it's not going to work, but 99% of the time, what you do is you say, "Whenever you have a destination, somewhere you want to point to,

348
00:33:03,550 --> 00:33:07,230
you tag it.

349
00:33:07,230 --> 00:33:17,750
You give it an ID, and then in the body of the book, you can say, 'Cross-reference to this target.' And if it is an ebook, it will generate a hyperlink for that.

350
00:33:17,750 --> 00:33:22,950
If it's a paper book, it will go and have a look at that target and find a good name for it.

351
00:33:22,950 --> 00:33:27,350
So, if it's a section, it will say the section named and then the title.

352
00:33:27,350 --> 00:33:31,870
If it's a diagram, it will give you the figure number.

353
00:33:31,870 --> 00:33:36,950
If it's a line of code, it will actually give you, you know, line 27 of the code or whatever.

354
00:33:36,950 --> 00:33:45,030
And so, it will expand your cross-reference into a textual description, just as if it was a real paper book.

355
00:33:45,030 --> 00:33:49,790
And you already mentioned, I think, how people can handle code embedding or formatting.

356
00:33:49,790 --> 00:33:55,430
So, code would be a code tag in your PML, you said.

357
00:33:55,430 --> 00:33:58,550
Yeah, in PML it's a code tag.

358
00:33:58,550 --> 00:34:04,470
In Markdown, it's just a Markdown fenced block, and you put the language after the fence. Must be backtick things, isn't it?

359
00:34:04,480 --> 00:34:06,830
In the backticks or tildes, yeah.

360
00:34:06,830 --> 00:34:19,270
And so, it will take that and it will do syntax highlighting on it and then generate whatever is necessary to format that either for the ebook or for the PDF.

361
00:34:19,270 --> 00:34:30,230
The PDF typically, we syntax highlight to black and white, but we actually also have books that are in color, printed in color, in which case the syntax highlighting survives as, you know, different colors.

362
00:34:30,230 --> 00:34:34,149
But we also have markup inside the code,

363
00:34:34,149 --> 00:34:43,869
where our tool chain is aware of all different languages and it knows about what constitutes a comment in each of those different languages.

364
00:34:43,869 --> 00:34:50,750
So, if you see for example, it would be a block with a slash star and then some text and star slash.

365
00:34:50,750 --> 00:34:53,190
If it was Ruby, it would be a pound character, a hash character.

366
00:34:53,190 --> 00:34:59,430
And it looks for markup inside comments to make it do things.

367
00:34:59,430 --> 00:35:03,990
So, that's where I can do things and say, "This is a destination.

368
00:35:03,990 --> 00:35:16,150
I want to put an ID on this line of code." And then in the body of the text, you can say, "As you can see on" and then reference that ID and it will say line 27 or whatever of the code.

369
00:35:16,150 --> 00:35:20,550
It's also how we do highlighting of or calling out particular lines of code.

370
00:35:20,550 --> 00:35:25,510
So, quite often authors want to show which lines changed as they're running through an example.

371
00:35:25,510 --> 00:35:33,590
And so, we have this markup where you can say, "Start emphasizing this code." And then "end." And it will actually put little arrows in the margin to show you that.

372
00:35:33,600 --> 00:35:46,030
And then finally, the really important thing is in the old days, what you would do is you would take the code that you wanted to describe and then cut and paste it into the inline text of the book.

373
00:35:46,030 --> 00:35:55,110
And the problem with that is that if you then went through and updated the code, how would you know that you'd correctly updated everything in the book as well?

374
00:35:55,120 --> 00:36:03,230
Because a particular chunk of code might occur on six different pages in the book as you're building up an example, say.

375
00:36:03,240 --> 00:36:14,550
So, what we wanted to do is to encourage authors not to put code inline in the text of the book, but to keep code as code in program source files.

376
00:36:14,550 --> 00:36:21,670
And one of the reasons we want them to do that is so that they can actually test that code as part of the book building process.

377
00:36:21,680 --> 00:36:34,190
So, for example, in my Ruby book, every single example of code in that book is actually run every time the book is built and it verifies that the output hasn't changed.

378
00:36:34,190 --> 00:36:42,830
So, that if for example, they make some change to Ruby and one of my examples no longer works, the book won't build and it'll tell me that that's that's changed.

379
00:36:42,830 --> 00:36:53,270
So, to make that work, you typically don't want every code example to have all of the code, you just want it to highlight one particular section as you're talking about it.

380
00:36:53,270 --> 00:37:01,150
So, one of the markups we can have in code is the ability to define regions of that code, sections of that code, and give them a name.

381
00:37:01,150 --> 00:37:11,230
And then when you include the code in your book, you say include from this file the sections named this, this, and this and it will just include those lines into the book.

382
00:37:11,240 --> 00:37:22,951
You mentioned already that our authors can build the book locally, so I guess the the whole tool chain is fairly smallish and you can get all set up locally. It can be.

383
00:37:22,951 --> 00:37:26,950
I was just wondering about your sort of the integration testing there of your own code.

384
00:37:26,950 --> 00:37:28,670
Is that something extra you need to do locally?

385
00:37:28,680 --> 00:37:32,590
Yeah, that takes a little bit of work, but it's not onerous.

386
00:37:32,590 --> 00:37:41,270
Like all of our editors, for example, well, most of our editors are English majors and most of them are set up to be able to build their on their own machines.

387
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:55,870
One of the joys of my life is when an editor whose prior taste of computing was using Microsoft Windows and Word gets online and says, "You know, well, I tried to set that environment variable and it didn't work."

388
00:37:55,870 --> 00:38:02,430
You know, it's kind of fun just that they can they're I'm corrupting a whole generation of editors there.

389
00:38:02,430 --> 00:38:06,870
But, yeah, setting the tool chain up typically involves you have a version control tool.

390
00:38:06,870 --> 00:38:09,350
We currently use Subversion for historical reasons.

391
00:38:09,350 --> 00:38:19,310
So, you would check out your repository and that gives you a whole bunch of stuff which is kind of like the boilerplate, but it gives you all of our tool chain as well.

392
00:38:19,310 --> 00:38:32,230
And so, given the repository, all you have to do typically is install a version of Java and that's because tools that do our XSL-FO processing are Java based.

393
00:38:32,230 --> 00:38:39,390
So, we took the advantage of having Java lying around and everything else we've got is based off that Java runtime.

394
00:38:39,390 --> 00:38:43,590
The tools are not written in Java, they're written in Ruby,

395
00:38:43,590 --> 00:38:53,990
but we run JRuby, which is a version of Ruby that runs inside the JVM and that lets us basically you have one dependency, which is this Java and then the rest just typically kind of works.

396
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:59,470
So, you obviously write your own tools for this because there was nothing before.

397
00:38:59,470 --> 00:39:01,430
Authors can build everything locally.

398
00:39:01,430 --> 00:39:11,910
If you want and you can put a bit more work in and do all your code examples and just for our listeners and myself, I have all my Rust and C code in individual Git repository.

399
00:39:11,910 --> 00:39:18,071
So, every time I make a change to code, I commit that and then commit it into the repository as well. Right.

400
00:39:18,071 --> 00:39:25,110
Actually, some of our authors also have an automated set of cherry-pick tools that they use with that.

401
00:39:25,110 --> 00:39:33,590
So, they'll have a Git repo, which represents each of the steps in an example they're building and if they discover they need to make a change to one of the early steps,

402
00:39:33,590 --> 00:39:40,710
they make that change and then they can make it ripple through all the later changes automatically, which is kind of cool.

403
00:39:40,720 --> 00:39:48,830
So, I'm presuming your distributors for the book and partners will also have a copy of this tool chain or do they get a slightly different one?

404
00:39:48,830 --> 00:39:51,750
No, we send them the result.

405
00:39:51,750 --> 00:39:58,190
So, as an author when you build, you get a kind of lightweight copy of the PDF or the ePub.

406
00:39:58,190 --> 00:40:00,870
When it's time for production,

407
00:40:00,870 --> 00:40:09,110
we take your book and we move it across to a protected section of the repository and at that point

408
00:40:09,110 --> 00:40:16,510
we get to use the kind of production version of the tools, which to be honest with you are about 90% the same.

409
00:40:16,510 --> 00:40:24,110
The difference is that we don't resample images, we normalize stuff, but we also make sure that all the color has been removed,

410
00:40:24,110 --> 00:40:33,871
and we convert everything to black and white, which to be honest with you, if you actually had a look at one of the PDFs we send off to the printer, you would probably wrinkle your nose at it. They look pretty ugly.

411
00:40:33,880 --> 00:40:40,510
They're a lot darker than you'd expect, for example, and that's simply because of the way the printing process works.

412
00:40:40,520 --> 00:40:47,550
It's taken us a long time to fine-tune that, but you have to manipulate things quite a bit to get it to work.

413
00:40:47,550 --> 00:40:54,430
And also, we have to send it off at a way, way higher resolution than we use for screen reading.

414
00:40:54,430 --> 00:41:02,550
Do you use any, or have any plans to use any AI tools, for example, for things like fact-checking, locating references for unreferenced content,

415
00:41:02,560 --> 00:41:13,990
checking that external references are properly used, i.e. links, or assessing content, you know, in the editing process for things like structure and clarity?

416
00:41:13,990 --> 00:41:21,430
So, yeah, I mean, the first, whatever, up until the thing where you said assessing content, we actually do that already, and we've done that for a long time.

417
00:41:21,430 --> 00:41:29,670
As part of the production process, we actually go through and validate all external links, and as part of any build,

418
00:41:29,670 --> 00:41:35,110
we actually validate all the internal links, and authors will get told if their links are invalid.

419
00:41:35,110 --> 00:41:46,950
We also validate things like, since the accessibility is important, we do things like make sure that all of your images have alt tags to describe them, and this kind of stuff.

420
00:41:46,950 --> 00:41:52,430
So, all that kind of stuff is done, but on the AI front, here's the thing.

421
00:41:52,430 --> 00:41:56,430
We have a rule that you cannot use AI to write the content.

422
00:41:56,440 --> 00:42:05,390
So, as an author, feel free to use AI as a research tool, or as a sounding board, or as whatever you want,

423
00:42:05,390 --> 00:42:17,190
but we actually run AI checkers on the books that were given by an author, just to make sure that it does not have AI generated content.

424
00:42:17,190 --> 00:42:24,470
And the reason for that is that we think the value we bring in the AI age is that our books don't hallucinate.

425
00:42:24,480 --> 00:42:29,910
All right, our books are the result of a human being giving it their best.

426
00:42:29,910 --> 00:42:36,310
And so we don't want our authors to be using AI to generate content.

427
00:42:36,310 --> 00:42:46,550
Now, at the same time, we do have something we we released fairly recently, a couple months ago, called the Pragmatic Assistant.

428
00:42:46,550 --> 00:42:59,750
And that is a ChatGPT project that has been trained on probably about 40 separate pieces of documents and memos and all sorts of stuff about what it means to be a bookshelf book.

429
00:42:59,750 --> 00:43:05,830
So we you know, it has all of our writing style guidelines, mockup information, all that kind of stuff.

430
00:43:05,830 --> 00:43:16,230
And we encourage authors to use that, particularly when they're starting out, because we have a style, we have a voice that we like people to use.

431
00:43:16,230 --> 00:43:21,830
And if you've never written long-form before, it can be quite tricky to get that right.

432
00:43:21,830 --> 00:43:24,950
And so in the old days, that used to be what the development editor would do.

433
00:43:24,950 --> 00:43:38,470
You would submit your book and the development editor would go, "Oh no, no, no, no, I like that." Now you can basically cut and paste a chapter or a whole chapter or a paragraph or something into the AI and it'll come back and say, "You really shouldn't use the passive voice here.

434
00:43:38,470 --> 00:43:47,791
That should be second person, not first person." Or actually, it would probably just say, you know, "Don't use I, use we or you here." And this kind of stuff.

435
00:43:47,791 --> 00:43:56,750
And so we're still refining that, but we're finding authors are quite liking that as a kind of angel on their shoulder kind of AI.

436
00:43:56,750 --> 00:44:01,230
Yeah, I've used that myself when I had to prepare the audience statement for Oh yeah, yeah.

437
00:44:01,230 --> 00:44:05,190
the first round of submission.

438
00:44:05,190 --> 00:44:09,190
Opened up the ChatGPT Pragmatic Program Agent, I think it was chatbot or something.

439
00:44:09,190 --> 00:44:13,511
Pasted what I'd written and it went, "Oh, no, no, no, no, no." Yeah.

440
00:44:13,520 --> 00:44:26,230
"I know how to do this." And he rolled up his sleeves and went, "No, this is how it should look." And I was like, "But that doesn't sound like me." Yeah, and that's the thing is it's not like, you know, we're not handing control over to an AI.

441
00:44:26,230 --> 00:44:33,310
It's purely I mean, I use it all the time as a kind of I don't know about you, but I always find I get my best ideas by describing them out loud.

442
00:44:33,320 --> 00:44:34,990
Yeah, the rubber ducky.

443
00:44:34,990 --> 00:44:38,270
The rubber ducking, right.

444
00:44:38,270 --> 00:44:40,190
And an AI is a fantastic rubber duck.

445
00:44:40,190 --> 00:44:45,310
One of the cool things we discovered about this is, you know that chat GPT, right?

446
00:44:45,310 --> 00:44:49,511
It always starts off by saying, "What a wonderful human being you are." Right?

447
00:44:49,511 --> 00:44:54,790
"That was really, really good, Dave." And we discovered that people didn't like that, right?

448
00:44:54,790 --> 00:45:02,670
If they submitted a section to be critiqued, they didn't want it to come back and say, "That was fantastic.

449
00:45:02,670 --> 00:45:09,830
I really enjoyed reading that." And then the next sentence says, "But of course, it was rubbish." You know?

450
00:45:09,830 --> 00:45:16,830
And it took us a long time to work out how to tell it not to be artificially encouraging.

451
00:45:16,830 --> 00:45:21,870
And I think we've kind of got that under control now.

452
00:45:21,870 --> 00:45:29,711
And the response I get from the authors is that so much better that they actually get all they get back is what they wanted, which is a critique of what they did. That's cool.

453
00:45:29,711 --> 00:45:31,630
I look forward to using more of those as they come out.

454
00:45:31,640 --> 00:45:33,310
Yeah.

