00:50:49,470 --> 00:50:53,590
But, one thing I haven't asked you about is do you require like multi-passes?

516
00:50:53,590 --> 00:50:58,550
So, say for example, I've linked to page 50 and we're on page 10.

517
00:50:58,550 --> 00:51:04,711
Does that just go in like a lookup table type thing and then you check it at the end or how does that work? That is a very good question.

518
00:51:04,720 --> 00:51:15,390
In early versions of the toolchain, we actually had to make multiple passes and that actually turned out to be really, really difficult.

519
00:51:15,390 --> 00:51:25,110
We actually had some pathological books where if you say it said, "As you can see on page whatever, right?"

520
00:51:25,110 --> 00:51:34,510
And if that page number was two characters, 99, then the line would break in a certain way and the rest of the book would format through in that going to that.

521
00:51:34,510 --> 00:51:36,710
If the page number changed to 100,

522
00:51:36,710 --> 00:51:43,950
then that would change the pagination of the book and that in potentially would then change the page number 100

523
00:51:43,950 --> 00:51:53,310
and it could change it back to 99 and then the 99 would then change it back to 100, and it would just loop forever changing these page numbers back and forth.

524
00:51:53,310 --> 00:51:55,870
So, we don't do that.

525
00:51:55,870 --> 00:52:06,030
Instead, what happens is cross-references get, like you say, they they get built up into effectively into a table, and then they're plugged in towards the end.

526
00:52:06,030 --> 00:52:09,350
And the formatting then basically adjusts based on that.

527
00:52:09,350 --> 00:52:13,390
So, there are still pathological cases, but it's all handled internally.

528
00:52:13,390 --> 00:52:16,310
That is a joy.

529
00:52:16,310 --> 00:52:19,230
Yeah, cuz I I did it myself when you're free to address anything.

