562
00:55:14,790 --> 00:55:23,670
and has page numbers and they will actually literally type in, you know, the name, the content and the page number on which it occurs into their own index program.

563
00:55:23,680 --> 00:55:29,310
And after they finish going through the whole book, their indexing program then kicks out the index.

564
00:55:29,310 --> 00:55:36,711
And of course, that's great until you then go and add content to the book in the second printing and it changes the page numbers. So, we don't do that.

565
00:55:36,711 --> 00:55:43,550
We actually you mark up the the same text that the author is working on with these index tags.

566
00:55:43,550 --> 00:55:49,990
And then, as part of the build process, it automatically collates those and creates the index at the end.

567
00:55:50,000 --> 00:55:55,390
So, when you get to handle change history or things like that, are all those tags there?

568
00:55:55,390 --> 00:56:00,830
You just have to be conscious of that or Yeah, that is a downside.

569
00:56:00,830 --> 00:56:05,310
The indexes are In the old days, they used to be like, you know, I'm going to index this word.

570
00:56:05,310 --> 00:56:08,070
And so, they would add it right in the middle of a paragraph.

571
00:56:08,070 --> 00:56:12,990
And now we tell them, if the paragraph's not too long, stick it at the beginning of the paragraph.

572
00:56:12,990 --> 00:56:16,670
And that way it doesn't interfere with the reading for the rest of the paragraph.

573
00:56:16,670 --> 00:56:23,070
But, the benefits of doing it that way, I think, far outweigh the ugliness that it includes in the document.

574
00:56:23,070 --> 00:56:25,110
Sounds like there's humans in the loop for the whole book process.

575
00:56:25,120 --> 00:56:27,470
Oh, absolutely.

576
00:56:27,470 --> 00:56:35,110
We have You have your development editor, who's basically doing the daily keeping an eye on what's going on.

577
00:56:35,110 --> 00:56:44,110
Then, after you submit a goes through a number of internal reviews here, it goes out to an external copy editor,

578
00:56:44,110 --> 00:56:48,750
an external indexer, and then it goes back to the author one or two times.

579
00:56:48,750 --> 00:56:51,070
It then goes to an external layout person.

580
00:56:51,070 --> 00:56:53,031
Another person does the cover. And then it will come together.

581
00:56:53,040 --> 00:56:54,870
It goes off to the printer.

582
00:56:54,870 --> 00:56:58,790
Yeah, it's very much a high-touch process.

583
00:56:58,790 --> 00:57:05,550
Before it gets that final stage, how does the feedback work between your all the different editors and the author?

584
00:57:05,550 --> 00:57:10,151
Is that stay within the document or is it email or how's that? It's both.

585
00:57:10,151 --> 00:57:12,430
We have markup.

586
00:57:12,430 --> 00:57:17,950
I'm sure you've encountered it where an editor can insert tags, the editor tag and an author or add.

587
00:57:17,950 --> 00:57:20,230
And an author can insert tags.

588
00:57:20,230 --> 00:57:24,830
And these are basically like callouts that you can put in your text.

589
00:57:24,830 --> 00:57:32,990
So, if you wanted to ask your editor in a question, you could say, "Should I say it this way or that way?" And you put that into a tag in the book.

590
00:57:32,990 --> 00:57:39,270
And then, when the editor builds the book those tags are highlighted both in the book itself that's produced but also during the build process.

591
00:57:39,270 --> 00:57:52,670
They can see a a list of questions that you sent come out during the build and then they can answer those however they see fit but quite often they'll answer those by putting those tags back in the book and the copy editor does the same thing.

592
00:57:52,670 --> 00:57:57,190
So the copy editor mostly works autonomously.

593
00:57:57,190 --> 00:58:05,110
We've experimented with having them do all the basically just put questions in and then have the author make the changes.

594
00:58:05,110 --> 00:58:07,350
99% of the time the copy editor is right.

595
00:58:07,350 --> 00:58:15,470
So instead what we do is we say to them if it's just obviously like bad grammar or something just fix it and if it's something

596
00:58:15,470 --> 00:58:23,070
you don't know or don't understand or are suspicious about then add one of your copy edit tags to the text at this point and then as an author

597
00:58:23,080 --> 00:58:34,270
when you get the book back you will have these copy editor questions in it and one of your jobs is to go through and basically remove those and fix whatever it was they were pointing at.

598
00:58:34,270 --> 00:58:47,311
We've got the sort of build chain CI CD and Bob that actually make a digital book as it were but then the people make the proper book. Basically, yep.

599
00:58:47,311 --> 00:58:57,830
I mean we still use that build chain to actually create yeah the mechanics of it but yeah it's not something currently that you can automate.

600
00:58:57,830 --> 00:59:03,070
Even the layout surprisingly there's a lot of aesthetic and experience that goes into it.

601
00:59:03,080 --> 00:59:05,150
Perfect.

